Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 22:13:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 35/71] Report what building a prompt removed from history

The turn-scoped filters are silent. Reasoning retention writes a verdict nobody
reads back, the shrinker leaves a marker inside text, and the drop pass returns
one index. None of it can be priced against a real session, so the claim that
`shrink_completed_outputs` is dormant rests on reading the code rather than on
data.

`for_prompt_annotated` already computes everything a counterfactual needs and
then throws it away. Return it as a `ContextFilterReport` instead: where the
prefix broke and what re-prefilling from there costs, what was dropped and on
which of the two grounds, what survived, and a `ShrinkReport` of what shrinking
removed. `shrinkable_before_break` is the opposite column -- output the
allowlist recognised but the cache gate refused to touch -- which is the number
that settles whether widening the allowlist could pay.

Adding those sizes back onto the prompt that was sent reconstructs the prompt an
unfiltered harness would have sent, so no second model call is needed. Nothing
about the request changes: `for_prompt` keeps its signature and discards the
report, and the drop pass now evaluates its predicate once per item instead of
twice.

Reasoning is measured with `reasoning_retention::reasoning_tokens`, not the
shared estimator, which reports reasoning as zero bytes and would silently price
the most expensive thing dropped here at nothing.
